  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nAn anomalously strong mid-level ridge over the central CONUS \nwill anchor omega blocking across much of North America over the\nnext several days. Meanwhile, an elongated surface ridge \nextending from the Lower Mississippi River Valley to the \nCanadian Maritimes is in the processes of congealing with \nanother expansive high pressure shifting toward the Upper Great \nLakes. As a result, quiet weather with unseasonably warm \nconditions will continue through the upcoming weekend. As was \nobserved the past few mornings, generally clear skies and \nlight/calm winds should promote shallow fog across primarily \ninterior northern Illinois late tonight through sunrise \nThursday.\n\nThe blocking pattern will slowly break down early next week as \nthe ridge flattens and a southwestern upper-level low begins to \nmeander toward the central CONUS. A series of smaller troughs on\nthe periphery of a developing longwave trough across the north \nPacific and western Canada will eventually pick up the \nsouthwestern low. Consensus guidance suggests that the influence\nof the low on our area could occur as early as Monday night, \nbut more likely Tuesday and Wednesday. Cooler and more \nseasonable conditions would accompany this system.\n\nKluber",
